@startuml sessions_and_config
title paddock: sessions and the synthesized config dir

allowmixing
set separator none
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0

package "sessions.py" {
  class Session <<dataclass>> {
    session_id
    name: unique among live sessions
    profile_name
    agent
    created_at: ISO 8601 UTC
    run_dir
    keep_alive
    pane_ids
  }
  class "functions" as SessionFns {
    list_sessions()
    get_session(name or id)
    create_session(profile, name): opens no pane
    attach(session, cwd) -> pane id
    launch(profile, name) = create + attach
    remove_pane(pane_id)
    launch_local(cwd) -> pane id
  }
}

package "backends/srt.py" {
  class "functions" as SrtFns {
    prepare(profile) -> Run
    load_run(run_dir) -> Run
    open_pane(run, label, cwd) -> pane id
  }
}

package "synth_config.py" {
  class "functions" as SynthFns {
    build(profile, agent, run_dir) -> dir, env, args, skill sources, missing
    discard_credentials(run_dir)
  }
}

database "$PADDOCK_STATE_DIR\n(default $HOME/.local/state/paddock)" as StateDir {
  file "sessions.json" as Registry
  folder "runs/<timestamp>-<rand>/" as RunDir {
    file "srt-settings.json" as Settings
    file "launch.json\ncommand + workdir + env" as Launch
    file "launch.sh, mode 0700\nthe command as a script, what every pane execs\nappends the launch's stderr to pane.log, then holds the\npane open when the command fails in its first 10 seconds\n(rewritten by load_run when an upgrade changes it)" as LaunchScript
    file "pane.log (+ pane.log.1 past 1 MB)\nthe stderr of every pane that ran this launch" as PaneLog
    folder "bin/: one symlink per ticked tool" as Shim
    folder "work/: scratch workdir" as Work
    folder "config/" as Config {
      file ".credentials.json -> the real key, or the agent's\nown login exported from the Keychain at 0600" as Creds
      file ".claude.json: a writable copy,\nminus every mcpServers key" as AgentJson
      file ".mcp.json: ticked servers only" as Mcp
      folder "skills/: ticked skills only" as Skills
    }
  }
}

database "the agent's real config dirs\nconfig_write_paths, e.g. $HOME/.claude" as RealConfig

SessionFns --> Session : builds
SessionFns --> Registry : written whole under a file lock,\nthen swapped in; a file that will not parse\nis reported and treated as empty
SessionFns --> SrtFns : create_session -> prepare\nattach -> load_run + open_pane
SessionFns --> SynthFns : remove_pane -> discard_credentials\nwhen the session is collected
SrtFns --> SynthFns : build the config dir
SrtFns --> RunDir : writes
SynthFns --> Config : writes
RealConfig --> Creds : symlinked: the agent only reads it
RealConfig --> AgentJson : copied: the agent writes it back
RealConfig --> Skills : only the names the profile lists

note right of Session
  One session, one settings file, one workdir.
  Every attached tab is a separate process tree
  under the same policy: srt has no guest for a
  second process to join (SPEC section 3.2).

  remove_pane drops the session when its last
  pane closes, unless keep_alive is set. The run
  dir is left on disk either way, minus the
  credential file: it may be an exported token,
  and that does not outlive the session.
end note

note bottom of RealConfig
  With a redirection (Claude Code today) these
  directories are in denyRead and denyWrite, so
  the skills and MCP servers nobody ticked are not
  there to read. srt checks the path a symlink
  resolves to, so the settings allow what the
  config dir links to back by name: the key and
  the ticked skills, read-only. What it copied is
  denied on the host instead (SPEC section 4.3).
end note

note bottom of Config
  Writable inside the sandbox; the run dir
  around it is not. CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR points
  here, via `tab create --env` and the sandbox
  command itself.
end note

note bottom of PaneLog
  Appended, because every tab on this run writes
  to it. `paddock logs <session>` prints the end
  of it, with a warning: this is the agent's own
  output, unfiltered, and paddock's no-secrets
  rule covers only its own log, which is elsewhere
  in $PADDOCK_STATE_DIR/logs/paddock.log.
end note

@enduml
